          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What actually happens in the airways during an asthma flare-up?",
          "answer": "During an asthma flare-up, several physiological changes occur in the airways:\n\n1. **Inflammation**: The lining of the airways becomes inflamed. This inflammation makes the airways more sensitive to various triggers, leading to increased symptoms.\n\n2. **Airway Constriction**: The muscles surrounding the airways can tighten, a process known as bronchoconstriction. This tightening restricts airflow and makes breathing more difficult.\n\n3. **Mucus Production**: The secretion of mucus increases, further obstructing airflow. The excess mucus can lead to coughing and wheezing.\n\nThese changes result in symptoms such as wheezing, breathlessness, and chest tightness, which can vary in intensity and may worsen in response to specific triggers (source: \"Asthma\" in the general clinical reference). \n\nIf you experience severe symptoms such as difficulty breathing, it is critical to seek immediate medical attention.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
